!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/32/ltr8/http/api-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  A description of an HTTP API whose payloads are TSON documents -- what OpenAPI is for JSON, minus
  the part OpenAPI mostly is.

  OpenAPI is large chiefly because JSON has no schema language, so it embeds one and carries
  components/schemas along with every document. TSON already has published, identity-addressed,
  immutable schemas, so this references them instead: an operation names a schema by !!id and a root
  type within it, and that is the whole of a contract.

  That pair is not invented here. It is what everything handling a TSON payload independently needs
  -- a writer emitting !!schema needs both, a reader against a stated schema needs both, and a JSON
  body carries neither so the header supplies one and the route the other. An API description is
  just: per operation, which pair goes in, and which pairs come out.

  Three things follow from schema references being real identities, which OpenAPI cannot do cleanly.
  Versioning falls out: an operation names order-1.tn or order-2.tn, rather than naming a component
  whose name encodes a version. An operation MAY pin the exact bytes of its contract with a
  ?sha256= reference (§2.2.1). And an error response references a type composed on problem-2.tn
  (§5.8), where OpenAPI needs allOf.

  A description is itself a TSON document governed by a TSON schema, so it is served, validated and
  versioned by exactly the machinery it describes.

  DELIBERATELY ABSENT from this revision: security schemes, request/response headers beyond
  parameters, callbacks, links, examples, and content negotiation across several media types. Each
  is a real part of describing an API and none is needed to state a contract; adding one is a new
  version under a new name (§10), never an edit.
  """
{
  http_method => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  @doc:"Where a parameter is carried. Not BODY: a body is a document, and has its own reference."
  parameter_location => !enum [PATH QUERY HEADER]

  @doc:"""
    A parameter carried outside the body.

    type names a scalar type from the standard library -- text, int32, uuid, date -- and is text
    rather than a type reference on purpose: a URL segment or a header value cannot carry a record,
    so a full (schema, type) reference here would invite contracts nothing can honour. This is the
    one place where TSON's document-orientation does not reach, and stating the limit is better than
    papering over it.
    """
  parameter => {
    name: non_empty_text
    in: parameter_location
    type: non_empty_text
    required: boolean
    description: text?
  }

  @doc:"""
    One TSON payload: the schema that governs it and the root type within that schema.

    Both are required, for the reason they are required everywhere else -- a schema alone does not
    say which of its types the document is, and a bound record writes no type-ref of its own.

    schema MAY carry a ?sha256= pin, which fixes the exact bytes of the contract rather than merely
    its name. media_type is absent for application/tson; state it for a body that is not TSON, such
    as JSON governed by a TSON schema through the TSON-Schema header.
    """
  body => {
    schema: uri
    type: non_empty_text
    media_type: text?
  }

  response => {
    status: int32
    body: body?
    description: text?
  }

  @doc:"""
    path is a template: a segment in braces names a PATH parameter, as in /orders/{id}. A server is
    free to route however it likes; this states the shape a client should expect.
    """
  operation => {
    method: http_method
    path: non_empty_text
    summary: text?
    parameters: [parameter]
    request: body?
    responses: [response]
  }

  @doc:"""
    base is where this API is served, and is absent when that is not fixed -- a description is about
    a contract, not a deployment, and the same contract may be served from several places.
    """
  api => {
    title: non_empty_text
    version: non_empty_text
    base: uri?
    operations: [operation]
  }
}
